A refining module now checks the termination of a call it inherits when the module closes a new call cycle through it (https://github.com/erniecohen/dafny/issues/409). A call between two members of one strongly connected component of the call graph must decrease. A refining module that gives a body to a member without one can put an inherited call into such a component. The refinement base made no termination check at that call, because there the callee did not call back, and the refining module assumed the check, because the call site is inherited. So a lemma could prove `false`, and a compiled method could recurse forever. An inherited termination check is now assumed only when its component is inherited unchanged: every member has a counterpart in the refinement base with the same decreases clause, and every call between two members is a call between their counterparts. Otherwise the inherited call is checked, with the error it gets in one module and the refining module as a related location, also when the base module comes from an included file or a library. The member, iterator or by-method body that holds the call is verified for its termination checks only: its other assertions are still assumed, as the base module proved them. A refinement that adds no body to a recursive component verifies as before. The one exception is a member whose base had no decreasable parameter: if the refinement defines such a parameter's type, the member's empty, inferred decreases clause gets inferred again, and its calls within the component are checked against the new clause. A base with an empty clause can prove only calls that need no decrease, such as a function's reference to itself in its own postcondition, so these checks hold where the base's did. The hidden option `--legacy-inherited-termination-checks` restores the old, unsound rule, for migration and comparison only.
